        Retatrutide (LY3437943) is the first peptide to simultaneously activate three incretin-axis receptors. UK researchers studying obesity pharmacology, hepatic metabolism, and triple-agonist pharmacokinetics use it to probe mechanisms that no dual agonist can isolate.
      </p>

      <div class="prose" style="max-width:none;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;">Mechanism — three receptors, one molecule</h3>
        <p>Retatrutide is a synthetic 39-amino-acid peptide that activates the GLP-1, GIP, and glucagon receptors simultaneously. The glucagon component is the key differentiator: while GLP-1 and GIP synergistically enhance satiety and insulin sensitivity, glucagon receptor activation increases hepatic energy expenditure through elevated lipid oxidation and thermogenesis.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;">Phase II evidence (NEJM 2023)</h3>
        <p>Jastreboff et al., <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em> Vol. 389, pp. 514-526 (2023), DOI: 10.1056/NEJMoa2301972. Randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled. 338 adults across 25 US centres, 48 weeks. The 12 mg arm achieved a mean weight reduction of -24.2% — the strongest effect ever documented for a single peptide in clinical research. Over 90% of the 12 mg cohort reached &ge;5% weight reduction.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;">Pharmacokinetics</h3>
        <p>Half-life ~6 days via C18 fatty acid modification (albumin binding), enabling weekly subcutaneous dosing in clinical trials. Phase III TRIUMPH programme (NCT05929066) ongoing at 45+ centres worldwide.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;">Storage and reconstitution</h3>
        <p>Supplied as sterile lyophilised powder. Store at -20&deg;C, protected from light. Reconstitute with 1-2 mL of bacteriostatic water (0.9% benzyl alcohol) for a working concentration of 5-10 mg/mL. Reconstituted solution stable at 2-8&deg;C for up to 28 days.</p>
      </div>

